---
import JournalLayout from '../layouts/JournalLayout.astro';

const lang = 'en';
---

<JournalLayout
  title="Aims & Scope | Ayamatma"
  description="Aims and scope of Ayamatma: Journal of Vedantic Studies."
  lang={lang}
>
  <section class="section stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Aims & Scope</p>
    <h1 class="section-title">Aims &amp; Scope</h1>
    <p class="muted">
      The journal exists to make Vedāntic thought precise, citable, and accountable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Aims</h2>
      <p>
        Ayamatma aims to publish work that clarifies Vedāntic concepts, examines Hindu philosophical
        traditions with rigor, and brings traditional categories into serious conversation with contemporary
        knowledge without reducing one to the other.
      </p>

      <p>
        The journal gives priority to work that defines its terms, distinguishes śāstric authority from
        philosophical inference, states its interpretive method, and shows why the question matters.
      </p>

      <h2>Scope</h2>
      <p>Suitable areas include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Advaita Vedānta, Viśiṣṭādvaita, Dvaita, and other Vedāntic traditions.</li>
        <li>Upaniṣadic interpretation and Sanskrit textual analysis.</li>
        <li>Bhagavad Gītā, Brahma Sūtra, prakaraṇa texts, and commentarial traditions.</li>
        <li>Hindu philosophy, Dharma, ritual, personhood, ethics, and religious practice.</li>
        <li>Consciousness studies, philosophy of mind, and the limits of reductionism.</li>
        <li>Science and Vedānta, when handled with conceptual discipline rather than loose analogy.</li>
        <li>Comparative philosophy and criticism of inadequate comparative categories.</li>
        <li>Translations, commentaries, review essays, dialogues, and methodological notes.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Out of scope</h2>
      <p>
        Ayamatma will not publish unsupported polemic, fabricated quotations, casual spiritual claims
        presented as scholarship, or articles that use modern science merely as decoration. Devotional
        writing may be considered only when its genre is clearly marked and its claims are not misrepresented
        as peer-reviewed research.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</JournalLayout>
